\CNK@Chapter{Lexical Analysis}\CNK@Label{chpt:lexical-analysis}

\CNK@Subchapter{Rockets Go Book!}

We explained how computers were programmed in the early days of computing in \CNK@Ref{chpt:binary-blisters}. We mentioned how the earliser computer programs were written in English; and we also mentioned how the first commercially viable compiler, \CNK@IndexWord{FORTRAN} was not advertised as a \emph{language}, rather, an \textbf{Autonomous Coding System}.

The biggest fault in FORTRAN was that it had no concept of ``whitespace''\CNK@Cite{}. This was not an oversight: index cards were precious and expensive. Tools like \CNK@IndexWord{Flex-o-Writer} (see \CNK@Ref{chpt:early-ttys}) were several years away. And programmers wanted freedom in formatting their code.

This led to several bugs that often, cost lives, more than it cost money. The classical example of FORTRAN treating whitespace as unnecessary afterthought was the \CNK@IndexWord{NASA Mariner 1} incidence.
